 **$20$** | **$10$** | **$5$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | $45$ | $54$ | $65$ | $77$ | $90$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03dd7eb-af5f-5f22-813b-7f3d5b84651c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400875} {:answer/id #uuid "ab86d5e2-989c-5d49-9b2d-7a183fd53e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400876} {:answer/id #uuid "da8626ef-a38f-59a8-a718-2155b988e445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400877} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe0f31-e9db-50f1-a23b-a66578e23d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400878} {:answer/id #uuid "1485ab74-1a6a-53e5-9aaf-e407a69b362d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab86d5e2-989c-5d49-9b2d-7a183fd53e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400876} :answer-field/id #uuid "d252fa00-ae50-5d73-8bce-ecc15224f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c1bd5a4-43fd-5d63-843c-209b976b49d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-140046" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & \\sqrt{6} & \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -10 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The $A$ is a triangular matrix, we obtain\n\n$$\n|A|=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 4=8\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has identical columns:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- Since $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, its determinant is:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=5\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot (-10)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5304084-5020-5bac-afe0-a4eaf6f0272d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Scale Factors and Angles of Rotation of Rotation-Scaling Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d416963d-0812-5442-bfb3-5143f8304cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400882} {:answer/id #uuid "25b68c79-4c84-5a8a-b3cb-c873ab4bbeec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400883} {:answer/id #uuid "399087ad-ae5c-50a9-b3f6-f634f021724e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400884} {:answer/id #uuid "abe1e0eb-b3fa-5749-9d80-faecfb2587cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400885} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b9e256-2bbe-5a6c-804a-deec95112ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe1e0eb-b3fa-5749-9d80-faecfb2587cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400885} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9bcdf0a-7ee5-512b-8b69-1c9ff761e1cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b946dc-503a-51e5-98d7-7e814f7abb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140401" :question/problem "Let $v$ be a non-zero vector from ${R}^{2}$ and let $A$ be the rotation-scaling matrix given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe description of $Av$ in terms of geometric transformations in the plane is given below. What are the missing entries?\n\n*To compute the vector $Av$, we rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\theta =\\text{_____}$ and then scale the result by a factor of $r=\\text{_____}.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can find the scale factor using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent $A$ as a product of a rotation and a scaling matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =6[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}r & 0 \\\\ 0 & r\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the missing entries are $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $r=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "289ae874-f555-50ee-896f-eb2188004124" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 3x3 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c340906-07d4-57b0-9046-295287f1840f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,0,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400889} {:answer/id #uuid "735c9554-2122-554a-9c6d-88f5c542af17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400890} {:answer/id #uuid "cf676211-b439-541c-a870-387de50e341e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,-2,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400891} {:answer/id #uuid "5f97e983-0e63-57dd-acda-842c7a7cfb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400892} {:answer/id #uuid "812db70d-b4af-5153-8bf4-a315c5a42142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {-3,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f97e983-0e63-57dd-acda-842c7a7cfb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\lambda \\in {3,4,6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400892} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed14d3f-a85e-59d0-a5e0-cd86b0195649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f772a19b-8ab2-5f26-9ef1-47231e01914d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140449" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b0f0ad-077e-5490-8903-2b75e6400fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400896} {:answer/id #uuid "c9962984-13d2-5e7a-a101-99ffb98df884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An imaginary ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400897} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a296a-362d-571b-bc2a-b4d7be4f7f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400898} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9951ca-7918-51ae-8e54-71674985b355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400899} {:answer/id #uuid "70050e40-2d4d-5f43-8504-d04583bd8a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9962984-13d2-5e7a-a101-99ffb98df884" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An imaginary ellipse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400897} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d7df124-dbc5-56d7-8581-3302e8d894bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9992e79-a8b1-5b6c-bb33-ddbd38d4ce02" :question/math-academy-id "q-140555" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4)+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-4 & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2, \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich defines an empty set. Notice that the equation is equivalent to $\\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(2\\text{i})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2}}=1,$ which has imaginary solutions, so we can interpret this as an imaginary ellipse with semiaxes $2\\text{i}$ and $\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abcdf400-bcb6-5e0b-9ec6-dab2bf54761d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $8$ bugs after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400903} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9c13c6-09fb-581a-8fa8-e20695647309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $27$ bugs after $5$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400904} {:answer/id #uuid "7235e376-c628-51f5-8028-177047d2588a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $15$ bugs after $4$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400905} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2d7c8e-9f10-5045-82f9-c14578f74b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $16$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400906} {:answer/id #uuid "6f60dfcf-9cc7-5683-856d-afa629682956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f60dfcf-9cc7-5683-856d-afa629682956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400907} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e151de4-60bd-5c74-b6e4-910b2f3a14f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cbfc231-381e-525d-b699-91d7448dd5be" :question/math-academy-id "q-140822" :question/problem "Scientists are observing a colony of bugs. The population $P(t)$ of the colony is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the number of bugs, and $t>0$ is the time (in days) since the observation started. \n\nAt what time is the population of the colony a minimum, and what is the number of bugs at this time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the population is a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0$ for $t>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$\nTo find the number of bugs when $t=2,$ we figure out $P(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =27+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27+[{x}^{3}-12x]{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =27+[(8-24)-(0)] \\\\  & =27-16 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $11<27$, we conclude that the minimum population is $11$ bugs, and this happens after $2$ days."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1bee282-2775-59f5-a929-9dc5327703bc" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Quadratic Function Over the 2D-Space to the Canonical Form Using Orthogonal Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b279401-b2e9-52e3-8e8a-5ed7f4ab0fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400910} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9a7c28-297a-59f1-8324-969697fcb145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400911} {:answer/id #uuid "6872e2bc-f5d0-5fd1-8dae-d3fd4d09b23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400912} {:answer/id #uuid "432384f9-da06-525e-baa9-7350f4251ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400913} {:answer/id #uuid "67ba60db-8cbf-553b-b63c-195669a23aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6872e2bc-f5d0-5fd1-8dae-d3fd4d09b23b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400912} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9d3fb41-4434-5c99-a083-41daf1b7f0a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2172cdc2-fb35-5393-84ba-3c3dbc3c7926" :question/math-academy-id "q-140997" :question/problem "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3559bdb9-c007-500b-83db-63f2d91cd407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400917} {:answer/id #uuid "f1984a87-93e4-523e-80b4-a37c28b7dc5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400918} {:answer/id #uuid "3f15fef9-83c3-50e8-980b-72c67ab596d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400919} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c0001-2df6-56e0-ae3c-2dca7d79a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400920} {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806f142-c711-5da8-b1d4-68c8001a3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab70a1a-e04b-524f-9866-e86f88144126" :question/math-academy-id "q-141140" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b362831ba2248e03537551c412ae7537646dcd09f0d00a598905f0d52a258da9.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is a *simple* root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c694a7cb-1273-5aef-bab1-b00405cb06a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5<x<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400924} {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} {:answer/id #uuid "ee071af0-120e-5c4a-b72d-0c54e26d3687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400926} {:answer/id #uuid "395b066b-730c-5a11-889e-cc63987fd8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-1$ or    $x>5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400927} {:answer/id #uuid "3271e9c8-ddaf-5be7-852f-5b3fd22e072b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19263d22-de7b-5ef3-9798-b98080320005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x<-5$ or    $x>-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400925} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3297c0-8b61-583d-8e5e-b17352fc3208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66cebf22-0b82-5cf0-a87d-5c8eaa25290f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141187" :question/problem "![](images/46/46edcf2265f41b8fac7931802875e1b9849006834b3509a186ffc7acb510959f.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(-x-5)(x+1)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(-x-5)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=(-x-5)(x+1)\n$$\n\n  \n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e830af773829b1712c6a87955e74411b6ad9921ae1aea58029ca968ab354c0f1.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-5$ or    $x>-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931} {:answer/id #uuid "5e69372e-e868-57ad-81a9-235542604c47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3cf5dc-1a5e-5ca5-bb04-89cb5648d292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f4c2df-fdff-5c81-86c1-ed79a5590ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $-\\frac{2}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "ee524a7c-b00b-54ff-b0db-1379d3f0eb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d912e9d9-60dd-53f8-84b5-6a01262be9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges to $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83cb541-9ba7-558c-804e-5b78b4a327b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c5b5131-1801-5dfb-aff8-f50ca940df15" :question/math-academy-id "q-141310" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{3{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{(4)}^{n}}{{(-6)}^{n}} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{4}{6})}^{n} \\\\  & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with $r=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400938} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400941} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400945} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400946} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400947} {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400948} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e81c67-49e3-5deb-9f3d-4bfdd5f4f433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559c184-d951-52c2-ae9b-1fd6b5cd4853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52634bca-83b3-54b7-95a5-9e44e545d6f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400952} {:answer/id #uuid "01dbf8ef-0546-56c6-8042-b514ff678457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400953} {:answer/id #uuid "13883436-b2ee-5552-ac8d-52c81b763494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400954} {:answer/id #uuid "b001b5b2-84bb-5ff9-8a1e-ad85dd8dd376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400955} {:answer/id #uuid "34858f57-efb3-57e6-9b45-36ddb2034af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34858f57-efb3-57e6-9b45-36ddb2034af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400956} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc09fe3d-b285-5568-9b7d-7d325bd277b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9955f0fd-f850-5637-ae70-443f1b225d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-141738" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d2f7497d6a11b4ceb994755498ef49bf0ce414601ab812dc55555c769a076ec.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7059f97-c797-51ad-be0a-f96acec895ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400959} {:answer/id #uuid "1e18716e-efdf-5322-a98c-f7741d67d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400960} {:answer/id #uuid "028c6bbc-5127-5736-b3ba-9f94a0f28ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400961} {:answer/id #uuid "3daa75bf-f787-56ff-9dca-1b80759e9c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400962} {:answer/id #uuid "d4912560-949d-5a8e-9911-c20ce554555e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e18716e-efdf-5322-a98c-f7741d67d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71eae7c-91a7-5647-b98b-5b82bc79007f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8be6241f-30ce-53dd-8d31-e6a859245baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-141740" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f07f2751398a101b41bc2c923fcdd7f6a5144da00008904297c36b2d8d44a30.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above are concave up?" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graphs I and III curve upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbe80595-c807-506c-a356-566f1edb3402" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Less Than (Or Equal To) Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb10726b-626d-526b-b3f9-de6bad9f3f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400966} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f8c3f4-5711-584d-9263-fed31acf6c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400967} {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400968} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8d0e9c-0806-580a-b031-8a01a725abe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\le x\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400969} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a43c47-c698-531e-a0a3-0b2da98a2630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le -5$ or $x\\ge 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce75758-abe3-5789-8de5-5d18996c87a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400968} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2ce6b8-4640-53b8-b17b-3464a0fe587d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f59dad02-8b17-598a-a754-71bfff242018" :question/math-academy-id "q-142315" :question/problem "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+6x-5\\le 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+6x-5\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+5) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/7c/7cfa66b5aa6a3f29e7154e329926a47bbbb0eb45dce3251148986785f09ee383.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le 1$ or $x\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bfd97b-bea2-5ab6-9084-e9f94b5ae836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400973} {:answer/id #uuid "b14388b2-8f17-5b6d-bd37-dce823b24383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400974} {:answer/id #uuid "e285d986-7f75-5785-9aea-c3e63564aefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400975} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fd822c-1f8c-5028-b56a-da7d3e74b8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400976} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a6d2-71a6-5531-bc91-795371f646d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bfd97b-bea2-5ab6-9084-e9f94b5ae836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400973} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e2d2d25-c810-5851-af0b-d59a08144e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a2eced6-9cd5-5469-b3dc-6168e1ec698d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142915" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=P(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\,|\\,Y=4)=\\frac{P(X=x,Y=4)}{P(Y=1)}=\\frac{f(x,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal distribution of $Y.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $x\\in {0,1,2},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(0,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.25}=0.4 \\\\ P(X=1\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(1,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0.15}{0.25}=0.6 \\\\ P(X=2\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(2,4)}{{f}_{Y}(4)}=\\frac{0}{0.25}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebabf742-2b8d-5392-a077-f34e8f4bd034" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Mass Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24afd0b0-d6de-56bf-ae8f-4be3381c967b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400980} {:answer/id #uuid "f992bbff-d9b3-5a36-9c82-3cc33ed8858f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400981} {:answer/id #uuid "c365283b-bc38-5d73-b350-7938e68cf363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400982} {:answer/id #uuid "29ea203b-ba7b-591f-b3f5-324bd7be1407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.3$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400983} {:answer/id #uuid "98bfc8a2-c396-5e52-ace3-f6d5e9d34641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98bfc8a2-c396-5e52-ace3-f6d5e9d34641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400984} :answer-field/id #uuid "232c8a87-8c1b-58ee-be3e-6552e315bf0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d63f5e38-dd7f-5597-b7d6-4c39a2fe3945" :question/math-academy-id "q-142928" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nFind the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "The conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we're required to compute the following set of conditional probabilities:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)=P(Y=y\\,|\\,X=0)\n$$\n\nBy the multiplication law for conditional probability, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y=y\\,|\\,X=0)=\\frac{P(X=0,Y=y)}{P(X=0)}=\\frac{f(0,y)}{{f}_{X}(0)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability mass function of $X$ and $Y,$ and ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal distribution of $X.$\n\nRecall that the marginal distributions for $X$ and $Y$ correspond to the row and column totals, respectively. So, let's compute these:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.5$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, for $y\\in {0,2,4},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=0) & =\\frac{f(0,0)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.5}=0.4 \\\\ P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=2) & =\\frac{f(0,2)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.1}{0.5}=0.2 \\\\ P(X=0\\,|\\,Y=4) & =\\frac{f(0,4)}{{f}_{X}(0)}=\\frac{0.2}{0.5}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the conditional probability mass function can be represented by the following table:   \n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,0)$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1888cd0-9005-51e4-8f23-a9c29cc22081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400987} {:answer/id #uuid "f928b778-da6a-5b79-9658-dc18dc898f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400988} {:answer/id #uuid "143789ed-0846-5adc-8244-a09df213d77e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400989} {:answer/id #uuid "3941f316-7785-5f8b-8014-322641f53352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400990} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8de928-580b-5d72-8ae9-f3db96bd744c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{V},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3941f316-7785-5f8b-8014-322641f53352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4527b519-e235-578b-ba4e-e12b67a2bec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b30d46be-646e-5361-a604-5fb66c710b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143034" :question/problem "The value $V$ of a particular art object increases with time. The rate of change of the object's value is proportional to the square of the value. What differential equation describes the change of the value at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **value** $V$ of a particular art object **increases** with time. The **rate** of change of the object's value is **proportional** to the **square of the value**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *value* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *value squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}∝{V}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the value *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=k{V}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "344f1838-a027-5fd5-82da-17e9b4fcac2b" :knowledge-point/title "Further Translating Problems With Proportional Rates of Change Into Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3609cc0-6b67-5266-9b4a-2f29d8232471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k\\sqrt{H},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400994} {:answer/id #uuid "ef6cd918-68d8-52d0-9155-4d41f4fd2598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400995} {:answer/id #uuid "add74caf-c6d7-5a05-a30c-9b03f535871e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{{H}^{2}},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400996} {:answer/id #uuid "e048b22c-334e-593c-a1bb-448f1e70ae12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-kH,\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400997} {:answer/id #uuid "1b08abf8-2a85-5a9c-9728-5bed51d13ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b08abf8-2a85-5a9c-9728-5bed51d13ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2},\\,\\,k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400998} :answer-field/id #uuid "5afc3f62-82b2-5ca2-8452-04ff173cc875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae491dc9-9063-5d70-aa00-00c9f3a98d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-143037" :question/problem "Construction waste stored in a large pile is taken to a landfill. The height $H$ of the pile decreases with time. The rate of change of the pile's height is proportional to the square of the height. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the height at time $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** $H$ of the pile **decreases** with time. The **rate** of change of the pile's height is **proportional** to the **square of the height**.  *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *height squared.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝{H}^{2}.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *decreases* with time, our derivative should be *negative*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-k{H}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1df005b-79a7-5108-a856-5625e567e6ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401001} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e2d5da-8670-5e49-9815-d8ca8944853f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401002} {:answer/id #uuid "50d5da3e-822f-53d4-8c03-30602011d2c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401003} {:answer/id #uuid "78a853e7-8361-5bd7-86dd-a6c97855e4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401004} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6fd1bd-e8a9-5868-be9f-9b06301f3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6fd1bd-e8a9-5868-be9f-9b06301f3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3031e52c-a4c8-5e7c-bd98-d40e649d233d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fb709d8-90a5-57a1-95a3-b0285559ae32" :question/math-academy-id "q-149548" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y+2)^{2}.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/34/345665733b6554a7cea9b54a4ca4e93b6d478d67231cfaf3f6180cf0a75c4b1a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/40/4058b0f027297faf71756da7cad372b76aae980657f053b95318115889c1d614.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $1-x$ | $(y+2)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,-1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/fc/fc54a4044465d485d4eb55aa8dd81c0e952e8dd56528bc2874bcc67dcffbf3c8.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6177b568-97a0-5f84-8134-18b2017427a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401008} {:answer/id #uuid "825b5cbf-2e29-5716-8a33-a26e05345bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401009} {:answer/id #uuid "c60f0014-ba02-5163-a857-2124830c98bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401010} {:answer/id #uuid "f8eb0c0b-f6a6-50a4-a44e-7d150fa9f126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401011} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce2b948-cc0f-5fa8-9825-7b8309ae482b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c60f0014-ba02-5163-a857-2124830c98bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401010} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d7c60f-8a44-503d-b169-b9efe3a77416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "377570ea-bfa8-57c5-ba4b-badf50a02969" :question/math-academy-id "q-149594" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=-{x}^{3}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](images/93/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-{x}^{3}$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\n \nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/fa/fa7402608c175237bfed11ddf032d867a0685e2ad5cf31f848809f796b705780.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9ccfc2e-0db3-5e5a-a113-ee578e578636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401015} {:answer/id #uuid "46620d78-5b91-5c4a-8c6d-533ad78b344e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401016} {:answer/id #uuid "e0836e8c-818d-5b8d-afd0-edf4ef3be7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401017} {:answer/id #uuid "4b709ee7-e72e-5aa2-9c92-1da1a32be9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401018} {:answer/id #uuid "0d109ebf-7ff3-5eb1-926